ifying the food  webs that those parasites depend on  here's an example of one of those  species this is a tapeworm that uses  sharks as a final host and then goes  into that little Hawk fish for an  intermediate host and you can see that  in Palmyra where we have tons of sharks  not surprisingly there's lots of larval  shark tapeworms in those hawk fish all  right so we can go back to the food web  and I wanted to just show you another  scenario of sort of an alternative way  that things can work so let's say we  cause a free-living species to go  extinct but that free living species is  a predator on some other species so we  might see some indirect increases in the  prey of species that are removed and if  those prey have parasites than those  parasites might increase in abundance so  there are possible ways that reducing  biodiversity in a certain way might  result in increases in infectious  disease and here's an example from kelp  forests in Santa Barbara so we have  here's a very simplified view of the  kelp forest food web we have lobsters  that eat sea urchins keep sea urchins at  relatively low abundance and that  releases kelp from being grazed down by  sea urchins but throughout most of  Southern California the food chain looks  like this because we fish almost every  legal sized Lobster from the system  in many cases that leads to large  abundances of sea urchins and reduced  kelp and one of the consequences of that  is there's interactions between fishing  and bacterial epidemics and sea urchins  what we see is that this graph shows the  abundance of sea urchins in different  sites and the probability that we see an  epidemic at those sites and you can see  that basically below a very low density  of  we don't see epidemics and that  increases the more sea urchins that we  have and that's really basic  epidemiology about you know what we  would expect for hosts in close contact  have more efficient transmission of  directly transmitted diseases so here's  another way to look at it we can compare  the abundance of these organisms in  marine reserves compared to fished areas  marine reserves have lots of lobsters  compared to fished areas few errands  lots of kelp and epidemics of bacteria  really rare in reserves and really  common in fished areas so here's an  example of how fishing can increase  infectious disease and it has  consequences for the system because if  you have a really big epidemic this is  the prevalence of the disease during an  epidemic on the x-axis the population  growth rate of sea urchins the following  year tends to be very negative this is  just a way to show that these epidemics  can have high mortality and as a  response to an epidemic if we look at  what happens to the kelp in the system  we tend to see an increase in kelp  following massive epidemics so another  way to look at this is we take lobsters  out of the system when we fish them but  disease kind of fills in that vacuum  that's left by the missing lobsters all  right we want to know how general this  was so we went to the Galapagos where we  have a very similar type of food web  different players but necess  interestingly enough of different  parasites more interesting parasites  maybe ones that you haven't heard of  these are specialized gastropods called  new limits and they're parasitic on  echinoderms two different species one  that lives in the spine one that sucks  the gonads out through the test and the  expectation then is that these parasites  would follow the same sort of patterns  that we saw in Santa Barbara an increase  in response in fishing but one of the  differences in this food web are these  small crabs that shelter underneath the  spines of pencil urchins and the crabs  eat snails so this small change in the  complexity or increased  the complexity of the system makes the  food web look a little bit different and  now it becomes a little bit more  difficult to predict exactly what the  net effect of phishing is but again by  comparing marine reserves and fished  areas we can get some expectations of  the patterns and what we see is that  overall there tends to be a negative  effect of fishing on parasites in this  system very similar food web but that  one change completely reverses the  dynamics okay here's another case which  actually has some public health  relevance one of the more important  tropical diseases may have heard about  is called schistosomiasis it's a snail  vector disease in a lot of tropical  areas and we've been working on this  parasite in Senegal along the senegal  river basin and basically prior to the  late 80s  just as myiasis was really rare in the  Senegal River and then to improve  agriculture they put in a dam that  prevented the tide from going upstream  from the oceans called the d ama dam and  basically that was very successful in  allowing for the irrigation of sugarcane  fields along the river made some of  those landowner owners very rich and it  led to the most massive outbreak in  schistosomiasis that's ever been  recorded which is still ongoing and we  were interested to know whether the  putting this dam and had changed aspects  of the food web in ways that might have  favoured schistosomiasis  there's many changes of course that  occur with dams but one of the ones that  we were particularly interested in was  the fact that prior to the dam the  villagers used to fish these giant River  prawns which are now gone from the  system we can't find them there anymore  and one of the thoughts is that these  giant River prawns particularly when  they're small love to eat snails they're  a great source of both calcium for the  snail and for protein  and so we've been doing laboratory  experiments for instance where you take  the two main snail vectors it's just a  samaya system in tanks we let them start  growing in a population and then we can  compare without prawns where the snail  populations really increase or with  prawns where they hardly do anything  same for this snail vector here and what  we're particularly interested in is how  predation varies according to the size  of the prawn and here's why  in Senegal what the villagers really  need is they need a break from  schistosomiasis and they need a way to  make money and prawns are actually a  very lucrative fishery so in theory we  could use either aquaculture or  restoration to bring prawns back into  that system give the villagers something  to fish and perhaps also get snail  control as a result and it turns out  that the way that the prawn population  works is the very large prawns are  cannibalistic and they eat the small  prawns and the small prawns are the best  snail predators so the best way to get  snail control from a prawn population is  actually to fish the large prawns which  are the most valuable economically so  we're hoping that we can start to move  towards implementation at the moment  what we have is a field experiment where  we've gone to these four villages and we  start out by treating everybody in the  village for schistosomiasis we've given  prize equal there they're all cured and  this is a normal sort of treatment that  the health agencies do there the problem  being that within six weeks or so  everybody becomes reinfected because  they use the water as part of their  daily lives and their snails in these  areas where people do the laundry and  swim etcetera so we've set up big  enclosures and in at some of these  villages we have then gone and  introduced river prawns that we get from  Cameroon it's the same species and we  put them in and and then the idea is to  look and see how come back later and  look at the villagers and see how many  have become reinfected in these four  villages and so far  are the villages where we've introduced  bronze into those sites have much slower  rates of reinfection I just got news  yesterday that our suggestion to put a  fish ladder on the D AMA Dam to allow  prawns to reestablish their migration  with the estuary has been met with a lot  of positive feedback from the dam  Authority and the reason that's  important is that this prawn uses the  estuary to breed and that's what  essentially when you put a dam that  prevents the prawn from persisting but  with a fish ladder perhaps we can get a  natural population back in okay so I've  just given you examples about how  biodiversity loss can potentially  decrease infectious disease other  examples where biodiversity loss like  damming in this case might increase  infectious disease if you're familiar  with the literature there's a hypothesis  that's very popular amongst conservation  biologists called the dilution effect  and the idea is that there can be this  win-win relationship between  conservation and human health where if  we protect biodiversity that that should  reduce disease risk and there's some  interesting there's some logical aspects  of this hypothesis and our view was that  it had perhaps been embraced a little  bit too quickly because of its win-win  outcome and so we did sort of a thought  exercise of hypothesis generation and  what we wanted to do is to ask  systematically for important parasitic  diseases of humans where would we expect  biodiversity to increase infectious  disease or biodiversity to decrease  infectious disease so the first step in  this was to come up with a list of  parasites that we could work with and so  we the list that we work with excludes  viruses and bacteria but it includes  protozoans and major helmets and  basically to be on the list you had to  cause some sort of significant tap  ecology and people and at least  somewhere in the world be relatively  prevalent and so here's the list you  don't have to read any of it but that's  69 different parasitic diseases that we  went through one by one and then applied  about eight different criteria to ask  should we expect a positive or negative  relationship with biodiversity this  paper will be coming out in ecology  sometime soon and this is just the  summary and the tabulation of our  hypothesized response so perhaps this  was a surprising thing is that we would  expect a very large fraction to not be  related to biodiversity all these are  primarily going to be the directly  transmitted diseases that don't really  interact with other species in the  environment we do certainly see examples  of cases where we would hypothesize that  we will see more disease with  biodiversity loss but about three times  as many cases where we hypothesize that  we should see less disease with  biodiversity loss of course these are  hypotheses what we'd like to do or what  we'd like to see is that this would do  two things one is to get people to go  out and test these specific agents and  also to at least have a broader view of  the potential negative or positive  effects that infectious that  biodiversity can have on infectious  diseases alright this is the last  section in the talk it's pretty short  section this is a colleague of mine Mark  torchin and mark is holding a very large  crab called the European green crab and  that's this one this is a large crab  from Europe in comparison to the crab  from San Francisco Bay where this crab  is invasive the European crabs are all  tiny and so we've been interested in  trying to figure out why invasive  species do so well and one potential  reason for that is that when they invade  they leave many of their parasites  behind so the green crab was a model  system for us because it had been  introduced one two three four or five  different places around the world and we  could go to Europe and look at the  parasites in the native range and  compare it to replicate places where the  crab had been introduced and the native  range the crab  definitely has a lot of parasites as  indicated by the size of this pie and  several different species of parasites  as well when we go around the world and  and collect equivalent samples what we  find is that in many places there are no  parasites in South Africa also no  parasites and in other places a few  parasites the place where we have the  most parasites is on the east coast here  where green crabs have been here for  more than 150 years and not only are  they very close geographically but  they've had time potentially to  accumulate parasites so that's one  species that when introduced species  that seems to have fewer parasites were  its invasive and also performs very well  where it's invasive and we see good  evidence that at least some of these  parasites may be responsible for its or  the loss of some of these parasites are  responsible for some of its increased  performance we can also look at a review  of 26 animal taxa where we have good  data to compare parasite species between  the native range and the introduced  range and so on average for our 26  animal taxa there's about 16 parasite  species none where the animal is native  okay so that's sort of the average and  where these animals have been introduced  we say they only bring with them about  two of those species so this is an  example of introduced species escaping  parasites because perhaps they come over  as larvae or in small sample sizes and  you just don't sample the full  complement of parasites in that  introduced range but introduced species  pick up parasites in the new range they  acquire on average about four parasite  species it still doesn't make up for all  the parasites that they've left behind  in their native range and so we may have  some evidence for well it's my lab  meeting today that's nice  I'd much rather be here  so we see at least some general support  for the fact that this enemy release may  be a general phenomenon there's two  points I really wanted to make about  this the first is that if invasive  species are showing up without their  full complement of parasites that may  help them do better but the other thing  which I think is more important  potentially is that if every invader  brings with it two parasite species then  species invasions are three times bigger  than we think they are and some of these  parasite species that they bring we know  can be problematic for native species  and cause additional problems so what  does this mean just this is the last  study I'll talk about how to do with a  food web of an Arctic lake that we've  been putting together in Norway and this  is an example of what that food web  looked like we think before about 1940  the free living species are indicated by  these boxes here that run across in the  main part of the web and free living  predator prey links are in black  parasites are up here on the right hand  column and their interactions the host  parasite interactions are in red okay  and so it's a complex system parasites  add a lot to this complexity but the  Norwegians like in a lot of places added  two species of fish to their web they  added arctic char and stickleback and  this is what the food web looks like now  it looks a lot more complicated than it  did before  and it's not just because so they added  these two species to the web but we got  five more parasites that complete their  life cycle in the lake as a result in an  addition for bird species that could  feed in that lake as a result of these  introductions the main reason I wanted  to to put this slide in is that it sort  of addresses two points the first is  that yes introduced species can bring  parasites into a system but it also  emphasizes the point that increases in  biodiversity facilitate opportunities  for parasites to complete their life  cycles in systems  so let me conclude with the following  points the first is that stress can  increase or decrease parasites this was  really important to consider when you're  thinking about effects at the population  level the second is that climate  suitability actually should be climate  change I suppose should shift but not  necessarily increase the distribution of  infectious disease and that's fairly  contrary to what tends to be reported in  the literature in addition even though  climate becomes more suitable for  infectious diseases in cases where it  does become more suitable we don't  necessarily expect that parasites will  respond because there are other  important factors like economics that  may overshadow those climate effects we  hypothesis that biodiversity loss mostly  reduces parasites this is exactly the  opposite of what you'll find in most of  the conservation biology literature and  then finally invasive species both bring  and escape parasites and both of those  phenomenon are problematic in terms of  conservation biology thank you very much
 

